      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6 given that the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 6 is 1.\n\nFirst, let's denote the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 6 as \\( r \\). So, we can write:\n\\[\nn = 6a + r\n\\]\nwhere \\( a \\) is some integer, and \\( r \\) is the remainder which satisfies \\( 0 \\leq r < 6 \\). Given that the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 6 is 1, we have:\n\\[\nr = 1\n\\]\nNow, let's express \\( n + 2010 \\) in terms of \\( a \\) and \\( r \\):\n\\[\nn + 2010 = (6a + r) + 2010 = 6a + 2010 + r\n\\]\nSince \\( r = 1 \\), we substitute \\( r \\) with 1:\n\\[\nn + 2010 = 6a + 2010 + 1 = 6a + 2011\n\\]\nNext, we need to find the remainder when \\( 6a + 2011 \\) is divided by 6. Notice that \\( 6a \\) is already divisible by 6, so it does not contribute to the remainder. Therefore, we only need to consider the remainder when 2011 is divided by 6. To find this, we perform the division:\n\\[\n2011 \\div 6 = 335 \\text{ remainder } 1\n\\]\nThis means the remainder when 2011 is divided by 6 is 1. Hence:\n\\[\n6a + 2011 = (6a) + (2011 \\mod 6) = 6a + 1\n\\]\nTherefore, the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
