      "text": "When we find the remainder won't change if we add multiples of the divisor. When $n+2010$ is divided by 6, the remainder won't change unless the remainder of subtracting 2010 won't change. Notice that $2010$ can be expressed as $2010=5x6$ or $2010=5\\times6^2$. Hence, the remainder will not change after applying a multiplier that is a multiple of $6^2$. Therefore, the remainder of $n+2010$, when divided by 6, is the same as $n+1$ when divided by 6. Therefore, the remainder of $n+2010$, when divided by 6, is the same as the remainder of $n+1$ when divided by 6. Hence, when $n$ is divided by 6, the remainder of $n+1$ is $1+1=2$. The remainder of $n+2010$ is $\\boxed{2} $\n\nWas the mathematics problem above, successfully solved? Yes, the mathematics problem has been successfully solved. \n\nCheckout our other math solutions https://quizin.net/maths/",
