      "text": "To determine the remainder when \\( n+2010 \\) is divided by 6, we need to first understand the condition given about \\( n \\).\n\nGiven: When \\( n \\) is divided by 6, a remainder of 1 is given.\nThis can be expressed mathematically as:\n\\[ n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6} \\]\nThis means that \\( n \\) can be written in the form:\n\\[ n = 6k + 1 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nNow, we need to find the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6. Let's denote \\( n + 2010 \\) as \\( m \\), so \\( m = n + 2010 \\).\n\nFirst, substitute the expression for \\( n \\) into \\( m \\):\n\\[ m = (6k + 1) + 2010 \\]\n\nSimplify \\( m \\):\n\\[ m = 6k + 1 + 2010 = 6k + 2011 \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the remainder when \\( 6k + 2011 \\) is divided by 6. Notice that \\( 6k \\) is divisible by 6, so:\n\\[ 6k \\equiv 0 \\pmod{6} \\]\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[ m \\equiv 2011 \\pmod{6} \\]\n\nNext, we reduce 2011 modulo 6:\n\\[ 2011 \\div 6 = 335 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\]\nThis is because:\n\\[ 2011 = 6 \\times 335 + 1 \\]\n\nSo:\n\\[ 2011 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6} \\]\n\nTherefore, the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1} \\]",
